WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.900
Our smartphones are constantly collecting data

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.620
about us.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:08.260
From the language we use in our texts and social media

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.340
posts, to what we like to watch and listen to.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.240
Our data can alert us to unhealthy habits— lack of sleep,

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.200
too few steps, too much screen time.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.760
Now, some researchers want to use these data

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.540
to predict when someone might attempt suicide.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.400
For decades, scientists have  tried to suss out the risk

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.140
factors for suicide.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.180
They've identified some key predictors, but most of

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.560
these describe a person's long-term risk.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.320
So the problem with the known risk factors for suicide,

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.530
and things like mental illness, is that

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:45.020
they apply to a huge percentage of the population,

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:48.360
and most of us are not at immediate risk of suicide.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.640
The other issue is that they don't tend to change

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.400
very much from day to day,

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.040
whereas suicidal tendencies can.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.000
Mental health professionals can ask patients

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.460
about their suicidal thoughts and plans directly,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.480
but predicting a person's risk

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.540
still involves some guesswork.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.700
Therapists can't monitor at risk people every moment,

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:11.130
but maybe smartphones can.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:12.846
One research project called MAPS—

00:01:12.846 --> 00:01:15.290
Mobile Assessment for the Prediction of Suicide—

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:18.660
aims to turn research about suicidal behavior

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:23.160
into a smartphone app that can predict spikes and risk.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.680
The approach could be especially effective for young

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.020
people, whose social and emotional lives

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.979
are so wrapped up with their smartphones.

00:01:30.979 --> 00:01:33.860
Suicide among teens is increasingly common—

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.180
it's the second leading cause of death

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.420
among young people in the U.S.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:42.000
In a 2017 CDC survey of nearly 15,000 high

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.060
school students across the United States,

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.649
7.4 percent said they'd attempted

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:49.840
suicide in the last 12 months.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.100
In the MAPS study, teams consent to let an

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.880
app on their smartphones collect data

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.380
around the clock.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.980
They're relying on information from previous studies and

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:00.500
previous theories about suicide to tell

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:01.760
them where to look.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.740
By analyzing the language teens type,

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:07.680
their tone of voice, and their facial expressions,

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.700
the researchers will look for signs of psychological pain

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:13.069
that might increase their risk of suicide a

00:02:13.069 --> 00:02:15.760
A phone's activity tracking functions

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.240
can reveal disruptions in a person's sleep habit,

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.320
such as insomnia, which has been associated with

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.000
suicide in adolescents.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.340
Finally, MAPS will look for signs of

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.860
faltering social relationships, rejection, and bullying.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:31.780
They'll track how frequently a person

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:33.620
reaches out to their online contacts,

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.720
how diverse those contacts are,

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:39.760
and how often the communication is reciprocated.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.560
One thing that clinicians have observed

00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:45.000
about suicidal impulses, is that they're ephemeral.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.100
They tend to pass;

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.340
they fluctuate and spike, and then they go away.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.300
And so the idea with this app is that

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:51.960
if it could predict when you're in trouble,

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.680
or when you're about to be in trouble,

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.260
it could actually step in, and it could suggest

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:57.760
a coping strategy for you,

00:02:57.769 --> 00:03:00.300
it could encourage you to reach out to

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.420
your therapist, or if you wanted to set it

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:06.100
up this way, it could also contact that person directly.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.680
This research is still preliminary.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:10.609
We don't know yet whether the predictions

00:03:10.609 --> 00:03:12.580
from phone data will be accurate,

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:14.720
but tracing our digital footprints could

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:17.020
someday be a tool to guide people

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:18.900
when suicidal impulses are strong—

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.740
and ultimately save lives.

